% Liveness of a function in SSA form (docs/plans/plan_prolog.md, stage
% 9), over the facts of mini-ml -flow (Ssa_facts.mli): the textbook's
% backward dataflow, by instruction, and what SSA adds: a phi's operand
% is live at the end of the predecessor it comes from and not at the
% phi's block's start, where the phi itself is.
%   live_before(V, P), live_after(V, P)   at a point (an instruction, a block's end)
%   live_in(V, B), live_out(V, B)         at a block's edges
% The same sets as mini-ml's allocator's own (Alloc.liveness).
%
% others:
% In SSA form liveness is cheaper than the equations: a value has one
% definition, which dominates its uses, so it is live on the paths from
% each use back to it; Benoit Boissinot, Florian Brandner, Alain Darte,
% Fabrice Rastello and others (2008-2011) compute it by those paths or
% answer a "is it live here" query from the dominator tree. These
% rules keep the textbook's way, the phis aside, as Appel's "Modern
% Compiler Implementation" (1998) and Cooper and Torczon's
% "Engineering a Compiler" teach it.

% (a slot never written is the constant 0: it needs no place)
needed(V, P) :- use(V, P), \+ zero(V).

live_before(V, P) :- needed(V, P).
live_before(V, P) :- live_after(V, P), \+ def(V, P).
live_after(V, P) :- next(P, Q), live_before(V, Q).
live_after(V, T) :- last(B, T), live_out(V, B).

live_out(V, B) :- succ(B, S), live_in(V, S), \+ phi(V, S).
live_out(X, B) :- succ(B, S), phi(V, S), phi_arg(V, B, X), \+ zero(X).
live_in(V, B) :- first(B, P), live_before(V, P).
live_in(V, B) :- phi(V, B).
